raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper asks whether online political hostility on X/Twitter is associated with indicators of political engagement and its antecedents, rather than just deprivation or personality traits.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we examine a different possibility. Classical research on political behavior emphasizes that education, internal efficacy, political interest, political knowledge, and participation are central to democratic engagement",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Online political hostility is positively associated with indicators of political engagement (interest, knowledge, participation) and its antecedents (education, efficacy), suggesting engaged users may be more hostile.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the strongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "These findings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based explanations, as highlighted in prior work",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Hostile users are more active and have greater reach, posting more frequently and having more friends and followers than less hostile users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, the results show that engaged but hostile social media users do not simply cater to fringe audiences; they post more frequently and have more friends and followers.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "Users who express more online political hostility also post more frequently and have more friends and followers.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study uses two years of panel survey data combined with scraped behavioral data from 2,567 American X/Twitter users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Combining two years of survey and behavioral data from X/Twitter, we examine associations between online political hostility and indicators of political engagement and its antecedents among American users.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period was 2567.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Political hostility is measured using a Super-Unsupervised (SU) word embeddings approach validated against expert labels and other toxicity metrics.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our key measure of online political hostility is based on a word embeddings approach termed the Super-Unsupervised (SU) approach [60].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Analyses include cross-sectional OLS regressions, Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM), and count models for activity and reach.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, using cross-sectional and panel analyses, we estimate associations between online political hostility and several theoretically distinct indicators",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM).",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Indicators of political engagement, particularly political interest, knowledge, and online participation, are positively associated with online political hostility.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The associations were obtained after controlling for broad personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification. The largest associations are generally observed for the primary measure of political hostility",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "The associations with online participation and political interest are, for example, 0.236 (95% CI [0.186; 0.285]) and 0.306 (95% CI [0.247; 0.366]) for online political hostility",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The association between engagement and hostility is stronger among users with higher leader-directed affective polarization, especially for participatory indicators.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Formal interaction models (Fig 3) show the clearest support for online and offline participation, while the evidence is weaker or inconsistent for more distal antecedents such as education",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "for those low on affective polarization only four out of eighteen associations... are significant, whereas for those high on affective polarization fourteen out of eighteen are significant.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Within-person increases in online political engagement are temporally followed by higher levels of online political hostility.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement are followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Higher political hostility is associated with increased posting frequency and larger numbers of followers and friends.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above the mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the number of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The findings are observational and associational, not causal, as RI-CLPM models do not rule out time-varying confounding or reciprocal relationships.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "However, RI-CLPM estimates do not by themselves establish causal effects. Time-varying confounding, measurement error, and reciprocal relationships may remain.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "The findings should overall be interpreted as associational rather than causal.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The data comes from American X/Twitter users, so results may not generalize to other platforms with different affordances or norms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the data come from American X/Twitter users. This platform is a public, textbased, and highly political platform, and the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Big Five personality controls do not directly test specific antisocial traits like Dark Triad or sadism, which were not measured.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the utilized Big Five controls do not directly test personality-based accounts centered on more antisocial traits such as Dark Triad traits, sadism, or other antagonistic traits. Such traits were not not measured in the survey.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The measure of affective polarization is leader-directed, not party-directed, limiting the generalizability of moderation analyses.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our measure of affective polarization captures feelings toward party elites rather than toward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors position the study as extending a 'revisionist' perspective on political engagement that acknowledges its potential negative consequences, integrating this into the study of online politics.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, we extend and integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on online political hostility.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper disputes or builds upon the claim that online hostility is primarily explained by socioeconomic marginalization, status threat, or personality traits like sadism, arguing instead that political engagement is a key factor.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "One set of accounts emphasizes socioeconomic marginalization, status threat, and grievance. From this perspective, hostility may be connected to experiences of deprivation, exclusion, or perceived loss of social standing",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Psychological accounts focus on personality and emotion-regulation, including antagonistic traits, sadism, negative mood, and situational triggers of anger or frustration.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures. Instead, it asks whether online political hostility is also associated with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
